# How Taliesin works {.unnumbered}

You change one number in a `.tmd` file and hit save. The paragraph you touched
re-renders in place, and nothing else moves: your scroll position holds, the 3D
canvas two sections down keeps spinning, the Jupyter kernel stays warm. This book
explains how.

The companion [User Guide](https://guide.taliesin.sh/) is about *using* Taliesin; this one is about
*how it is built*, for contributors, for anyone extending it, and for readers who
want the reasoning behind its design decisions.

## A Rust core with thin clients

All of the intelligence lives in a Rust core, and every client is thin. The core parses a
`.tmd` into a block model, renders each block to HTML, and speaks a small, stable
websocket protocol. The browser preview is a consumer of that protocol (the VS Code
companion hosts the same preview), and the `build` command writes the same blocks to a
file, so a new client can be added without a rewrite.

The rest of this book explains what follows from that design:

- **Click-to-source.** Every block carries the source position it came from.
- **Block-level incremental updates.** A save ships only the blocks that changed, so
  live state survives the edit.
- **No per-edit startup cost.** A warm server and a warm Jupyter kernel, kept across
  saves.

## Our running example

To keep things concrete, the chapters follow one tiny document, `post.tmd`:

````markdown
# Cooling coffee

A fresh cup cools toward room temperature, the gap shrinking exponentially:
$T(t) = T_\text{room} + (T_0 - T_\text{room})\,e^{-t/\tau}$.

```{python}
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

t = np.linspace(0, 30, 200)
plt.plot(t, 20 + 70 * np.exp(-t / 10))   # tau = 10 minutes
plt.show()
```
````

`post.tmd` has three blocks: a heading, a paragraph with a line of math, and a Python
cell that draws a cooling curve. It touches every part of the system: prose and a
heading to render, math to typeset, code to highlight and to run, and an output to
splice back in. Across the book we watch it become blocks, get diffed, cached, and
pushed to the browser. When a chapter needs a concrete edit, we change the cooling
constant `tau` from 10 to 12.

This book is built with Taliesin, so @fig-cooling is drawn by running `post.tmd`'s
own code as this page renders, through the pipeline the rest of the book describes.

```{python}
#| echo: false
#| label: fig-cooling
#| fig-cap: "post.tmd's cooling curve, drawn by running its cell as this page was built."
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

t = np.linspace(0, 30, 200)
plt.plot(t, 20 + 70 * np.exp(-t / 10))   # tau = 10 minutes
plt.xlabel("minutes")
plt.ylabel("temperature (°C)")
plt.show()
```

## How to read this book

The chapters build on each other, roughly from the outside in:

- **[Architecture](architecture.tmd)**: the crates, the data flow, a save end to end.
- **[The rendering pipeline](rendering.tmd)**: raw text to a block model to HTML, with
  math and highlighting resolved offline.
- **[The block model](block-model.tmd)**: the structure the rest of the system depends
  on, and the websocket contract built on it.
- **[Code execution & the kernel](execution.tmd)**: the warm kernel, the execution plan,
  the persistent output cache.
- **[Extending Taliesin](extending.tmd)**: the conventions, and the one seam meant to be
  extended without touching the core.

If you read only one chapter, read [Architecture](architecture.tmd). It frames
everything else.
